1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,680
Welcome back to Equation Station SA, where we make Grade

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:07,760
Twelve Mathematics easier to understand, one step at a time.

3
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:11,180
Imagine a straight line and a parabola on one graph.

4
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:15,060
How do we calculate exactly where they meet, without guessing?

5
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:18,800
And what if the exam question doesn't even mention simultaneous

6
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:21,860
equations? Today we'll find out.

7
00:00:22,140 --> 00:00:25,620
We'll start with our free Algebra and Equations course, then

8
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:29,120
tackle a real six-mark question from the November twenty twenty-five

9
00:00:29,140 --> 00:00:32,640
National Senior Certificate Mathematics Paper One.

10
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:35,120
At the end, you'll try one yourself.

11
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:37,700
Lesson One gave us factorisation.

12
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:41,920
Lesson Two introduced the quadratic formula and nature of roots.

13
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:44,920
Lesson Three covered quadratic inequalities.

14
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:48,440
Now we'll connect those skills to find the coordinates where

15
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:52,680
two graphs meet. Simultaneous equations must both be true for

16
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:54,880
the same values of the unknowns.

17
00:00:54,980 --> 00:00:58,620
With x and y, the answers are matching pairs: each

18
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:01,080
x-value together with its y-value.

19
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:04,980
On a graph, they represent points lying on both graphs.

20
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:09,380
The key move is substitution: use the simpler equation to

21
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:11,360
replace one unknown in the other.

22
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:14,360
That leaves one equation in one unknown.

23
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:18,040
Solve it, then go back to find each matching value.

24
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:23,840
Our routine is recognise, rearrange, substitute, solve, and check.

25
00:01:24,260 --> 00:01:27,020
Let's use the exact example in the Grade Twelve Algebra

26
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:28,300
course on our website.

27
00:01:28,740 --> 00:01:32,060
The first equation is y equals x plus two.

28
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:34,740
The second is y equals x squared.

29
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:37,760
Both equations tell us what y equals.

30
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:41,760
So at an intersection, x plus two must equal x

31
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:45,740
squared. Write that as x squared equals x plus two.

32
00:01:46,060 --> 00:01:47,820
Now move everything to the left.

33
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:52,280
We get x squared minus x minus two equals zero.

34
00:01:52,520 --> 00:01:56,140
That looks familiar! It is a quadratic equation, just like

35
00:01:56,160 --> 00:01:58,040
the ones we solved earlier in this series.

36
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:01,880
Which two numbers multiply to negative two and add to

37
00:02:01,900 --> 00:02:05,340
negative one? Negative two and positive one.

38
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:09,260
So we factorise into x minus two, multiplied by x

39
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:11,000
plus one, equals zero.

40
00:02:11,320 --> 00:02:14,460
The zero-product rule tells us that x minus two equals

41
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:17,600
zero, or x plus one equals zero.

42
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:21,880
Therefore, x equals two, or x equals negative one.

43
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:23,520
But stop there for a moment.

44
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:25,820
Have we finished? Not yet.

45
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:28,040
The question asks for x and y.

46
00:02:28,060 --> 00:02:32,280
We still need the y-value that belongs to each x-value.

47
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:35,880
Go back to the simpler equation: y equals x plus

48
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:39,460
two. When x equals two, y equals four.

49
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:42,820
When x equals negative one, y equals one.

50
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:45,940
So our two answers are the point two, four, and

51
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:47,760
the point negative one, one.

52
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:50,920
On the graph, the straight line and parabola meet at

53
00:02:50,940 --> 00:02:52,780
precisely those two points.

54
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,180
Each coordinate lies on both graphs.

55
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:58,400
The algebra confirms what the picture shows.

56
00:02:58,860 --> 00:03:03,480
Quick exam warning: two x-values are not automatically two complete

57
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:07,140
answers. If you stop at x equals two or x

58
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:11,480
equals negative one, you have not given the corresponding y-values.

59
00:03:11,880 --> 00:03:15,680
A simultaneous-equations solution is a matching ordered pair.

60
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:20,320
Substitute each x-value back, find its own y-value, and present

61
00:03:20,340 --> 00:03:25,000
both coordinates clearly. In our example, two goes with four,

62
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:27,320
but negative one goes with one.

63
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:29,080
Never mix the pairs up.

64
00:03:29,220 --> 00:03:32,980
Your final answer must have both coordinates for each solution.

65
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:35,980
This one extra step can save marks.

66
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:38,640
Always check both original equations.

67
00:03:38,980 --> 00:03:43,240
For two, four: four equals two plus two, and four

68
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:47,900
equals two squared. For negative one, one: one equals negative

69
00:03:47,920 --> 00:03:51,400
one plus two, and one equals negative one squared.

70
00:03:51,420 --> 00:03:54,100
Each pair works in both equations.

71
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:57,700
Now let's step up to a real matric examination question.

72
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:02,020
I checked the November twenty twenty-five National Senior Certificate Mathematics

73
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:04,840
Paper One, Question One point Two.

74
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:06,520
It was worth six marks.

75
00:04:06,860 --> 00:04:09,940
Instead of showing us two equations ready to solve, the

76
00:04:09,960 --> 00:04:11,900
examiner gave us two statements.

77
00:04:12,380 --> 00:04:15,640
Our first job is to translate those statements into mathematics.

78
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:19,180
The first statement says that x is two more than

79
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:22,800
y. That means x equals y plus two.

80
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:24,480
There is our first equation.

81
00:04:24,780 --> 00:04:27,760
The second statement tells us that five times the product

82
00:04:27,780 --> 00:04:30,540
of x and y is six more than the square

83
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:32,520
of x. Let's unpack it.

84
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:35,940
The product of x and y is x times y.

85
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:39,100
Five times that product is five x y.

86
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:42,000
Six more than the square of x is x squared

87
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:45,720
plus six. So the second equation is five x y

88
00:04:45,740 --> 00:04:47,860
equals x squared plus six.

89
00:04:48,020 --> 00:04:51,620
Notice: the question never told us which method to use.

90
00:04:51,860 --> 00:04:54,680
We recognised two unknowns and two conditions.

91
00:04:55,180 --> 00:04:57,680
That's method recognition under exam pressure.

92
00:04:57,953 --> 00:05:01,673
The first equation is x equals y plus two.

93
00:05:01,993 --> 00:05:05,753
Rearrange it to make y the subject: y equals x

94
00:05:05,773 --> 00:05:09,433
minus two. Substitute this into the second equation.

95
00:05:09,833 --> 00:05:13,853
Five x, multiplied by x minus two, equals x squared

96
00:05:13,873 --> 00:05:18,873
plus six. Expand: five x squared minus ten x equals

97
00:05:18,893 --> 00:05:20,793
x squared plus six.

98
00:05:21,013 --> 00:05:24,533
Collect all the terms on one side: four x squared

99
00:05:24,553 --> 00:05:27,513
minus ten x minus six equals zero.

100
00:05:27,533 --> 00:05:30,963
Divide by two to get two x squared minus five

101
00:05:30,983 --> 00:05:33,333
x minus three equals zero.

102
00:05:33,713 --> 00:05:36,733
Now use our factorisation skill from Lesson One.

103
00:05:36,993 --> 00:05:40,623
Two x squared minus five x minus three becomes two

104
00:05:40,643 --> 00:05:44,893
x plus one, multiplied by x minus three, equals zero.

105
00:05:45,173 --> 00:05:49,013
Check the expansion: the middle term is negative five x.

106
00:05:49,273 --> 00:05:53,233
By the zero-product rule, two x plus one equals zero,

107
00:05:53,253 --> 00:05:55,593
or x minus three equals zero.

108
00:05:55,933 --> 00:06:00,413
Therefore x equals negative one half, or x equals three.

109
00:06:00,613 --> 00:06:03,673
But remember, we need matching x and y values.

110
00:06:04,073 --> 00:06:06,413
Return to y equals x minus two.

111
00:06:06,693 --> 00:06:10,053
When x is negative one half, y is negative five

112
00:06:10,073 --> 00:06:13,113
halves. When x is three, y is one.

113
00:06:13,273 --> 00:06:16,933
Our two simultaneous solutions are negative one half with negative

114
00:06:16,953 --> 00:06:19,333
five halves, and three with one.

115
00:06:19,573 --> 00:06:21,633
Always check the original conditions.

116
00:06:21,973 --> 00:06:24,953
For three and one, x really is two more than

117
00:06:24,973 --> 00:06:29,073
y. And five times three times one equals fifteen, while

118
00:06:29,093 --> 00:06:31,233
three squared plus six is fifteen.

119
00:06:31,533 --> 00:06:35,193
Both conditions hold. For negative one half and negative five

120
00:06:35,213 --> 00:06:37,613
halves, the first condition also holds.

121
00:06:37,953 --> 00:06:41,553
Five times x times y gives twenty-five quarters, and x

122
00:06:41,573 --> 00:06:44,333
squared plus six gives twenty-five quarters as well.

123
00:06:44,633 --> 00:06:45,853
So both pairs work.

124
00:06:46,193 --> 00:06:49,193
That's the six-mark November twenty twenty-five question.

125
00:06:49,573 --> 00:06:54,013
The marking guideline rewards the equations, substitution, quadratic form

126
00:06:54,033 --> 00:06:57,453
and matching values. Make your working visible to the examiner.

127
00:06:57,473 --> 00:07:01,133
Now for a third worked problem, but this time there

128
00:07:01,153 --> 00:07:04,433
is a twist. Must a line and a parabola always

129
00:07:04,453 --> 00:07:05,753
meet at two points?

130
00:07:06,193 --> 00:07:09,553
Consider y equals x squared and y equals two x

131
00:07:09,573 --> 00:07:13,413
minus one. Before we calculate, make a prediction: will there

132
00:07:13,433 --> 00:07:16,013
be two solutions, one, or none?

133
00:07:16,313 --> 00:07:17,473
Let us test it.

134
00:07:17,613 --> 00:07:21,333
Because both expressions equal y, set x squared equal to

135
00:07:21,353 --> 00:07:22,813
two x minus one.

136
00:07:23,033 --> 00:07:26,453
Rearrange to x squared minus two x plus one equals

137
00:07:26,473 --> 00:07:31,133
zero. Factorise: x minus one, multiplied by x minus one,

138
00:07:31,153 --> 00:07:34,333
equals zero. This is the same as x minus one,

139
00:07:34,353 --> 00:07:36,133
all squared, equals zero.

140
00:07:36,313 --> 00:07:38,673
Both factors give x equals one.

141
00:07:38,753 --> 00:07:41,133
That is only one distinct x-value.

142
00:07:41,573 --> 00:07:44,513
Substitute into y equals two x minus one.

143
00:07:44,613 --> 00:07:46,453
We get y equals one.

144
00:07:46,553 --> 00:07:49,673
Our only intersection is the point one, one.

145
00:07:49,793 --> 00:07:53,353
Check it: one squared equals one, and two times one

146
00:07:53,373 --> 00:07:55,133
minus one also equals one.

147
00:07:55,153 --> 00:07:58,893
Watch the line touch the parabola at that exact point,

148
00:07:58,913 --> 00:08:02,093
then move away; it does not cross twice.

149
00:08:02,533 --> 00:08:06,253
Remember Lesson Two? The discriminant here is zero, giving a

150
00:08:06,273 --> 00:08:10,153
repeated root. Do not invent a second different point just

151
00:08:10,173 --> 00:08:11,573
because you see two brackets.

152
00:08:11,973 --> 00:08:14,993
And if two graphs never meet, there are no real

153
00:08:15,013 --> 00:08:18,913
intersection points. The algebra and the graph must tell the

154
00:08:18,933 --> 00:08:24,253
same story. To practise, visit equationstationsa dot top, choose Grade

155
00:08:24,273 --> 00:08:26,913
Twelve, then Algebra and Equations.

156
00:08:27,333 --> 00:08:30,913
The free guided course includes worked examples, a Mastery Bank

157
00:08:30,933 --> 00:08:33,333
and an auto-marked knowledge test.

158
00:08:33,553 --> 00:08:36,493
Look for the free completion certificate opportunities on the website,

159
00:08:36,513 --> 00:08:41,073
too. For structured exam revision, our optional paid Equation Station

160
00:08:41,093 --> 00:08:44,453
SA Grade Twelve Student Book and Answer Book are available

161
00:08:44,473 --> 00:08:45,973
on the Study Guide page.

162
00:08:46,313 --> 00:08:49,773
They cover Paper One and Paper Two practice, worked solutions

163
00:08:49,793 --> 00:08:53,673
and revision. The free online lessons and practice remain available

164
00:08:53,693 --> 00:08:56,253
to everyone. Now your turn.

165
00:08:56,433 --> 00:09:00,233
Pause and solve this website practice: y equals two x

166
00:09:00,253 --> 00:09:04,333
minus one, and y equals x squared minus four.

167
00:09:04,593 --> 00:09:07,433
Find every matching pair, not just the x-values.

168
00:09:07,853 --> 00:09:12,653
Pause now. Ready? Set two x minus one equal to

169
00:09:12,673 --> 00:09:14,253
x squared minus four.

170
00:09:14,613 --> 00:09:19,053
Rearrange: x squared minus two x minus three equals zero.

171
00:09:19,073 --> 00:09:23,453
Factorise into x minus three, multiplied by x plus one,

172
00:09:23,473 --> 00:09:28,093
equals zero. So x equals three, or x equals negative

173
00:09:28,113 --> 00:09:32,253
one. Substitute into y equals two x minus one.

174
00:09:32,493 --> 00:09:34,813
If x is three, y is five.

175
00:09:35,133 --> 00:09:38,233
If x is negative one, y is negative three.

176
00:09:38,433 --> 00:09:42,673
The intersection points are three, five, and negative one, negative

177
00:09:42,693 --> 00:09:46,113
three. Check both pairs in both original equations.

178
00:09:46,493 --> 00:09:49,353
If you got stuck, replay the substitution step.

179
00:09:49,773 --> 00:09:54,233
Here's the exam routine: identify the simpler equation, rearrange if

180
00:09:54,253 --> 00:09:59,553
needed, substitute, solve the quadratic, recover the matching y-values, and

181
00:09:59,573 --> 00:10:02,373
check. Sometimes the graphs cross twice.

182
00:10:02,793 --> 00:10:04,213
Sometimes they only touch.

183
00:10:04,573 --> 00:10:07,773
Never assume every question must give two different answers.

184
00:10:07,793 --> 00:10:12,733
Practise this lesson on equationstationsa dot top, and explore the

185
00:10:12,753 --> 00:10:15,053
optional Study Guide books if you want more revision.

186
00:10:15,513 --> 00:10:17,813
Subscribe and share this with a matric learner who needs

187
00:10:17,833 --> 00:10:20,913
it. Keep practising, and I'll see you in the next

188
00:10:20,933 --> 00:10:22,673
Equation Station SA lesson.
